#!/bin/rc
# eccp — Plan9/APExp C++ compiler driver for EDG's C-generating front end
#
# Runs the pipeline:
#   cpfe (C++ -> C)  ->  cc -c  ->  [link against libedg.a]
#
# Usage:
#   eccp [flags] file.cpp ...        compile and link
#   eccp -c [flags] file.cpp ...     compile to .o, do not link
#   eccp -F [flags] file.cpp         cpfe only: write the generated C
#   eccp -K [flags] file.cpp ...     keep the generated .eccp.c intermediates
#   eccp -o out [flags] file.cpp ... compile, link, write to out
#
# `c++' IS THIS, AND `eccp' IS WHERE IT LIVES.  `rc/bin/ape/c++' is now a
# one-line forwarder to this script, so `CXX=c++' works for autoconf, for
# GNU make's own default and for `sys/src/cmd/transpilers'.  The body is
# here because `eccp' is UPSTREAM's name for this front end, which also
# retires a recorded trap: `cpfe --help' and `cpfe -V' answer
# `invalid option' precisely because those are the DRIVER's options, and
# there was no driver.  **The name was CFRONT's until cfront was removed
# from the tree**; one implementation under both names is what the
# forwarder buys, and it is the arrangement that cannot drift.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIVE THINGS IN HERE ARE LOAD-BEARING AND WERE EACH MEASURED.
#
# 1. `--target win64' IS NOT OPTIONAL.  cpfe is multi-target
#    (`src/target.c:6525' holds eight configurations) and
#    `src/fe_init.c:49180' defaults the index to 0, which is
#    `set_legacy_target_config' -- built from the UNSUFFIXED `TARG_*'
#    macros, i.e. the values the HOST that translated `target.c' had.
#    Without this flag cpfe refuses its own configuration:
#    `check_target_config: must use SoftFloat library', from
#    `target.c:10642' when `targ_ldbl_mant_dig == 64'.  win64 is EDG's
#    LLP64 configuration and is kencc's model exactly -- including
#    `targ_setjmp_func "setjmp"', which is the symbol the first link
#    reported undefined.  It is a BRIDGE: the end state is entry 0
#    carrying kencc's values, and then this flag goes.
#
# 2. `--clear_flag=use_predefined_macro_file' GOES WITH IT, and is not a
#    workaround.  With the target set, cpfe next wants
#    `lib_win64/predefined_macros.txt', which is where a real win64 build's
#    `_WIN32' and `_MSC_VER' come from and which we ship for no target.
#    Suppressing it is precisely *LLP64 without the Windows macros*, which
#    is what is wanted here.
#
# 3. THE THREE `--sys_include' DIRECTORIES, IN THIS ORDER.  The C++
#    language-support set first (`/sys/include/ape/c++', where `<new>'
#    resolves to `new.stdh' through cpfe's own `include_file_suffixes'),
#    then the ARCH directory before the generic one, which is APExp's own
#    recorded search order (`/$objtype/include/ape' shadows
#    `/sys/include/ape').
#    **A run with two of them is not a smaller version of a run with
#    three**: the first VM translation omitted `/sys/include/ape', which
#    is inert for a toy including no headers and inert for nothing else.
#    `include_c99' needs no entry of its own -- it held one file,
#    `iso646.h', and `/sys/include/ape/iso646.h' is ours now and is the
#    better of the two (guarded `#ifndef __cplusplus'; in C++ all eleven
#    are keywords).
#
# 4. `cc' RATHER THAN `pcc', AND THE NAME IS THE WHOLE REASON.
#    `sys/src/ape/9src/cc.c' is the single line
#    `#include "../../cmd/pcc.c"', and `pcc.c:75' is
#    `objext = (strcmp(prog,"cc")==0) ? "o" : ot->o' -- **one binary, and
#    the name it is invoked under chooses the object extension.**  Under
#    `cc' it is `.o' on every architecture, so this driver needs no
#    objtype-to-suffix table and the object names it computes are right
#    on all eleven.  bacon's recipe paid a round for exactly this.
#
# 5. `--instantiate local' IS WHAT MAKES A TEMPLATE LINK AT ALL HERE.
#    Without it the first toy with a template translated cleanly, compiled
#    cleanly and then failed at the link:
#
#	main: undefined: _Z5twiceIiET_S0_
#
#    which demangles to `int twice<int>(int)'.  That is not a fault in the
#    translation: under EDG's DEFAULT mode a template instantiation is
#    emitted by NO translation unit, and a separate `edg_prelink' pass
#    decides which object shall carry each one and re-runs the front end.
#    *We have no prelinker and mk cannot naturally express one*, so the
#    default can never link a template on this target -- the tree's own
#    notes already measured this from the other end, 556 of 558 undefined
#    `_Z' names in cpfe's own link being instantiations emitted nowhere.
#
#    `local' is upstream's answer for a target with neither a prelinker
#    nor COMDAT: every instantiation a translation unit needs is emitted
#    INSIDE it, with internal linkage.  Nothing is left undefined and
#    nothing can collide, so it settles both halves of the link model at
#    once -- the half `-C' exists for and the half `-C' could not touch.
#
#    THE COST IS REAL AND IS NOT CODE SIZE.  Internal linkage means each
#    object gets its OWN copy, so a template's static data member, or a
#    static local inside a template function, stops being one object
#    shared across the program and becomes one per translation unit.  A
#    program whose correctness rests on that identity -- a counter, a
#    registry, a `std::once'-ish guard -- is silently wrong rather than
#    loudly broken.  Nothing in this tree has measured it, and a single
#    translation unit (which is every use so far) cannot show it.
#    `--instantiate used' is one word away for anyone who wants the
#    default back and has a prelink step to go with it.
#
# 6. EVERY `--opt=value' IS QUOTED OR BUILT BY CONCATENATION.  In rc `='
#    is a SYNTAX CHARACTER, legal only as an assignment at the start of a
#    command or inside quotes -- so a bare `--sys_include=/sys/include/ape'
#    is `token '=': syntax error', not an argument.  That is this tree's
#    `awk -v name=$name' rule, and `sys/src/external/edg/NOTE' said it bit
#    only in an mkfile recipe; it bites in an rc script just the same, and
#    the NOTE is corrected.  `'--sys_include='^$d' is the idiom
#    (`rc/bin/fdwatch' and `rc/bin/bash-runtests' already use it).
#
# EXCEPTIONS AND RTTI ARE OFF BY DEFAULT, deliberately.  EDG itself is
# built `-fno-exceptions -fno-rtti', so that is the only configuration this
# port has ever exercised, and `lib_src''s setjmp-based EH has never been
# run.  `-fexceptions' and `-frtti' turn them on and are the honest way to
# find out.  *A default that matches what has been measured is not the same
# as a default that matches what the compiler can do*, and this one says
# which it is.
#
# Environment overrides:
#   CPFE     path to the cpfe binary      (default: cpfe)
#   CC       path to the C compiler       (default: cc -- see note 4)
#   CXXINCL  C++ language-support headers (default: /sys/include/ape/c++)
#   EDGLIB   EDG's C++ runtime            (default: /$objtype/lib/ape/libedg.a)
#   STLINCL  extra C++ include root       (default: empty)
#   EDGSTD   language level               (default: --c++14)

rfork e

if(~ $CPFE '')    CPFE    = cpfe
if(~ $CC '')      CC      = cc
if(~ $CXXINCL '') CXXINCL = /sys/include/ape/c++
if(~ $EDGLIB '')  EDGLIB  = /$objtype/lib/ape/libedg.a
if(~ $EDGSTD '')  EDGSTD  = --c++14

fn usage {
	echo 'usage: eccp [-cFvK] [-o out] [-I dir] [-D sym] [-U sym]' \
	     '[-l lib] [-L dir] [-fexceptions] [-frtti] file.cpp ...' >/fd/2
	exit usage
}

# ---- parse arguments ----
cflag    = ''	# -c: compile only, skip the link
fflag    = ''	# -F: cpfe only, write the generated C
vflag    = ''	# -v: echo each command
kflag    = ''	# -K: keep the generated .eccp.c files
ehflag   = ''	# -fexceptions
rttiflag = ''	# -frtti
outfile  = ''

iflags  = ()	# -I... -D... -U..., handed to BOTH cpfe and cc
lflags  = ()	# -l... -L..., linker only
ccopts  = ()	# -g -O etc, cc only
xxopts  = ()	# --anything else, passed through to cpfe verbatim
sources = ()	# .cpp .cxx .cc .C
cfiles  = ()	# .c, compiled with cc directly
objects = ()	# .o .a

args = $*
while(! ~ $#args 0) {
	a = $args(1)
	args = $args(2-)
	switch($a) {
	case -c
		cflag = 1
	case -F
		fflag = 1
	case -v
		vflag = 1
	case -K
		kflag = 1
	case -fexceptions
		ehflag = 1
	case -frtti
		rttiflag = 1
	case -fno-exceptions
		ehflag = ''
	case -fno-rtti
		rttiflag = ''
	case -o
		if(~ $#args 0) usage
		outfile = $args(1)
		args = $args(2-)
	case -I -D -U
		if(~ $#args 0) usage
		iflags = ($iflags $a^$args(1))
		args = $args(2-)
	case -l -L
		if(~ $#args 0) usage
		lflags = ($lflags $a^$args(1))
		args = $args(2-)
	case -I* -D* -U*
		iflags = ($iflags $a)
	case -l* -L*
		lflags = ($lflags $a)
	case -o*
		outfile = `{echo $a | sed 's/^-o//'}
	case -g -O -O0 -O1 -O2 -O3 -Os -w -Fw -FV -A -B
		ccopts = ($ccopts $a)
	case --*
		# anything else beginning `--' is cpfe's own and is passed
		# through.  A `=' in one has to be quoted by the CALLER, for
		# the reason in note 5 -- rc cannot see one otherwise.
		xxopts = ($xxopts $a)
	case *.cpp *.cxx *.cc *.C
		sources = ($sources $a)
	case *.c
		cfiles = ($cfiles $a)
	case *.o *.a
		objects = ($objects $a)
	case *
		echo 'eccp: unrecognised argument:' $a >/fd/2
		usage
	}
}

if(~ $#sources 0 && ~ $#cfiles 0 && ~ $#objects 0) usage

# ---- the C++ header directory must exist, and say so BY NAME ----
# Its absence does not fail here: it fails deep inside cpfe as
# `cannot open source file "new"', which reads like a fault in the program
# being compiled rather than in the installation.  *Name the precondition
# instead of letting the consequence name itself* -- four silently-failing
# preconditions in bash's own test harness is what that rule cost.
# `new.stdh' rather than the directory, because an EMPTY `c++' directory
# would pass a `test -d' and fail exactly the same way.
if(! test -f $CXXINCL^'/new.stdh') {
	echo 'eccp: no' $CXXINCL^'/new.stdh' >/fd/2
	echo '      APExp is not mounted, or $CXXINCL is wrong' >/fd/2
	exit noincl
}

sysincl = ('--sys_include='^$CXXINCL)

# An extra C++ include root goes AFTER the language-support set and BEFORE
# the C headers, so a header-only library cannot shadow `<new>' and
# friends.  `$STLINCL' is EMPTY by default: the imported
# `sys/src/external/paragon_portable_stl/include' is `namespace
# portable_stl' rather than `std', so it is a corpus to translate rather
# than a standard library to install -- see `$CXXINCL/README'.
if(! ~ $STLINCL '') \
	sysincl = ($sysincl '--sys_include='^$STLINCL)

sysincl = ($sysincl '--sys_include=/'^$objtype^'/include/ape' \
	            '--sys_include=/sys/include/ape')

ehopts = ()
if(~ $ehflag '')   ehopts = ($ehopts --no_exceptions)
if(~ $rttiflag '') ehopts = ($ehopts --no_rtti)

cpfeopts = ($EDGSTD $ehopts --target win64 \
	    '--clear_flag=use_predefined_macro_file' \
	    --instantiate local \
	    $sysincl $iflags $xxopts)

# ---- running a stage ----
# `$status' IS CLOBBERED BY THE VERY NEXT COMMAND, which cost `cmd/c++lib'
# forty zero-byte translation units and a build that reported success:
# every failure arm there read `if(! ~ $status ''){ cleanup; exit $status }'
# and `cleanup' succeeded first, so `exit' saw the empty string.  Capture
# into `st' before doing anything else, always.
st = ''
generated = ()

fn die {
	echo 'eccp: FAILED at' $1 'on' $2 '--' $st >/fd/2
	if(~ $kflag '' && ! ~ $#generated 0) rm -f $generated
	exit eccpfail
}

fn run {
	if(! ~ $vflag '') echo $* >/fd/2
	$*
}

# ---- stage 1: cpfe, C++ -> C ----
# `--gen_c_file_name' rather than a redirection, for the OTHER half of the
# c++lib bug: a shell redirection CREATES the target whatever the command
# then does, so `mk' sees a recipe that succeeded and marches on.  cpfe
# writes the file itself and leaves none behind on a failure.
objs = ()
for(s in $sources) {
	stem = `{echo $s | sed 's/\.\(cpp\|cxx\|cc\|C\)$//'}
	gen = $stem^'.eccp.c'
	if(! ~ $fflag '' && ! ~ $outfile '') gen = $outfile
	run $CPFE $cpfeopts '--gen_c_file_name='^$gen $s
	st = $status
	generated = ($generated $gen)
	if(! ~ $st '') die cpfe $s
	if(~ $fflag '') {
		run $CC -c $ccopts $iflags $gen
		st = $status
		if(! ~ $st '') die $CC $gen
		objs = ($objs `{basename $gen .c}^'.o')
	}
}

# -F stops here: the generated C IS the product, so it is kept whatever
# -K says.
if(! ~ $fflag '') exit 0

# ---- stage 2: any plain .c the caller handed us ----
for(c in $cfiles) {
	run $CC -c $ccopts $iflags $c
	st = $status
	if(! ~ $st '') die $CC $c
	objs = ($objs `{basename $c .c}^'.o')
}

if(~ $kflag '' && ! ~ $#generated 0) rm -f $generated

if(! ~ $cflag '') exit 0

# ---- stage 3: link ----
# `$EDGLIB' is named explicitly and LAST: EDG's runtime holds the operator
# new/delete families, the vtable and RTTI helpers and the static-init
# machinery, and a kencc static link resolves archives in the order
# recorded, so it has to come after everything that can reference it.
# libap arrives after it from `pcc''s own `#pragma lib' handling.
#
# **THE STATIC-INIT GAP IS OPEN**: nothing calls `_Z12__call_ctorsv', so a
# translated program's static constructors do not run.  `lib_src/main.c'
# defines the helper and is compiled with `-D_main=__edg_main' because
# `_main' is `6l''s default entry point.  See `sys/src/external/edg/NOTE'.
out = $outfile
if(~ $out '') out = a.out
run $CC -o $out $objs $objects $lflags $EDGLIB
st = $status
if(! ~ $st '') die link $out
exit 0
